  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nCool, dry surface high pressure will continue to build over the\nregion, while a series of disturbances will bring a steady flow\nof mid-level clouds over much of the Quad State today. This may\ncontinue into Monday over southeast Missouri. A few light \nshowers may produce sprinkles at a few locations today. \n\nOver the Evansville Tri State area and portions of the \nPennyrile, more sunshine is expected and strong mixing may take \nrelative humidity down to 30% or less. Thankfully, winds will \nbe light, so there should be no major fire weather concern.\nTemperatures will remain below normal today and Monday.\n\nThe surface high begins to loosen its grip on the region Tuesday\nand modest south winds will kick in on Wednesday leading to a\nsignificant warmup. Temperatures should reach normal Tuesday and\nthen well above normal Wednesday and Thursday, when highs in \nthe mid to upper 80s will be common. The 00Z GEFS has a 50-90% \nchance of reaching 90 over most of the region, while the NBM is \na bit more conservative, in the 10-50% range. Moisture will be \nlagging the heat a bit, with dewpoints climbing to around 60 \nWednesday and then into the mid to upper 60s on Thursday.\n\nThe general consensus of the 00Z guidance is to delay the onset\nof rain chances to late Wednesday night into Thursday, or\npossibly as late as Friday. The initial chances will still be\nmainly over southeast Missouri and southern Illinois. A slower \narrival certainly makes sense with that southern stream system \nbeing positively-tilted. In fact, much of the 00Z guidance lets\nthe upper low sit over Texas Thursday into Saturday, then brings\nit northeast through the Quad State next Sunday and Monday.\nExpect the onset of PoPs to trend later with future forecast\nupdates.\n\nThe NBM has PoPs beginning Wednesday night and continuing every\nperiod through next Sunday. There will be little, if any \ninstability until the upper system arrives next weekend, so QPF \nshould be light until then. A cold front should enter, if not \npass through the region late in the week. However, with the lack\nof instability it is not likely to be a focus for thunderstorm \ndevelopment. \n\nOnce the system arrives, there is some potential that it will \nbring a healthy surface low northeast right through the Quad \nState. This could lead to some severe weather and heavy rainfall\npotential. That said the NBM's QPF averaging around 3\" over the\nentire Quad State Wednesday night through next Sunday is way \noverdone in comparison to the latest guidance. \n\nA stronger cold front will come in behind the system Sunday and\nbring an end to the precipitation and usher in more cool air.",
